<p>Port Neches is a Mid-County city on the west bank of the Neches River in Jefferson County, between Port Arthur, Nederland, and Groves. It is a close-knit residential community with neighborhoods that sit near major refining and petrochemical facilities, and those facilities employ many local families. If your Port Neches home or business has been damaged by a hurricane, tropical rain, a thunderstorm, a fire, or a burst pipe, a public adjuster in Port Neches can help you read your policies, document the loss, and present a complete claim. Port Arthur Public Adjuster serves Port Neches as part of our Jefferson County service area.</p>

<h2>Storm exposure in Port Neches</h2>
<p>Port Neches shares the weather of the rest of Mid-County. According to the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2017Harvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service in Lake Charles</a>, Hurricane Harvey produced widespread storm totals over 40 inches across Jefferson County in late August 2017, with the highest report, 60.58 inches, near neighboring Nederland. Record flood crests were observed on the lower Neches River, and more than 64,000 homes flooded countywide. The NWS also noted that several refineries in the county took on floodwater and were offline for an extended period, which shows how a storm can affect businesses as well as homes.</p>
<p>Port Neches property owners also dealt with Hurricane Rita's widespread wind damage in 2005, Hurricane Ike in 2008, Tropical Storm Imelda's extreme rainfall in 2019, and the damaging winds Hurricane Laura brought to Jefferson County in 2020, when trees and power lines came down across the county. Look up reports for a particular date with the <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a>.</p>

<h2>Insurance issues for Port Neches homeowners</h2>
<h3>TWIA, homeowners, and flood</h3>
<p>Because Jefferson County is in the TWIA catastrophe area, many Port Neches homeowners carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA policy for wind and hail, and a separate flood policy. A single hurricane can therefore create two or three claims, each with its own adjuster, deductible, and deadlines. TWIA claims generally must be reported within one year of the date of loss. See <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> and <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>

<h3>Hurricane deductibles</h3>
<p>Wind and named-storm deductibles are often a percentage of the dwelling limit, so a 2% deductible on a $200,000 home is $4,000. Know your number before a storm with the <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a>.</p>

<h3>Repairs and windstorm certification</h3>
<p>Roof repairs and re-roofs in Jefferson County generally need TDI windstorm inspection and a WPI-8 certificate to keep TWIA eligibility, along with any city permit. Plan for inspections before the work starts. Whether your policy pays for code or windstorm upgrades depends on ordinance or law coverage; our <a href="/local-building-codes/">building codes page</a> explains the connection.</p>

<h2>Living near industry</h2>
<p>Port Neches neighborhoods sit close to large industrial facilities, and that creates a few claim scenarios other towns see less often:</p>
<ul>
